From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 05:45:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Request the AOP and MTP DAPF
 handoff

m1n1 hands a T8132 coprocessor's DMA filter (DAPF) over only when the OS
device tree lists the coprocessor in /chosen
linux-enablement-mac,dapf-handoff. The J604 DT lists none, so an m1n1
that hands the J604 AOP over would disable it, and the AOP (microphones,
headphone jack) never gets its firmware memory-region.

Request two consumers:

- /arm-io/aop: m1n1 checks that the AOP is halted, programs the AOP
  DART's DAPF bank (instance[1]), reserves the firmware segments from
  iop-aop-nub segment-ranges and the DAPF windows, and adds them to the
  AOP's memory-region; on any failure it disables the AOP. The J604 ADT
  matches the J713 one for every value this uses: dart-aop instance[1]
  is DAPF bank 0 and dapf-instance-0 (16 slices) is byte-identical in
  the 25G83 and 26B123 templates, and the J604 27.2 runtime ADT has the
  same bank and the same five AOP segments (__TEXT, __DATA, __ETEXT,
  __EDATA, __OS_LOG). Until m1n1 admits the J604 on its AOP path this
  request has no effect: the AOP keeps no memory-region and the AOP
  driver fails its probe before touching it, as today.

- /arm-io/mtp: for a requested MTP that is powered, T8132 m1n1 only
  checks the DART's instance table and preserves the filter iBoot set
  (it does not write T8132 DAPF banks other than the AOP's). Requesting
  it matches the J713 profile, and dart-mtp dapf-instance-0 is
  byte-identical on J604 and J713, so the request stays correct if that
  ever changes.

/arm-io/pmp is not requested, and there is no
linux-enablement-mac,dapf-power-on-handoff: m1n1 would power the PMP on
and leave it on, but no J604 node uses the PMP yet, and its DART has no
DAPF bank (DART only in the templates, a GAPF window at runtime).

This is the J604 counterpart of the J713 /chosen block in
aurora-silicon/linux 332eea1e90 ("arm64: apple: activate J713 with its
PMGR ownership policy"), without its PMP entries and without
linux-enablement-mac,disk-boot, which neither the omacom m1n1 nor its
U-Boot reads.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index 3cf8202f47..d66c0b5840 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -29,6 +29,19 @@
 		wifi0 = &wifi0;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * The coprocessors whose DMA filters m1n1 hands over (dapf.c,
+	 * kboot.c dt_set_t8132_aop()). The AOP's handoff programs its DART's
+	 * DAPF and reserves its firmware segments and DAPF windows; an m1n1
+	 * that hands the J604 AOP over disables it when this does not ask
+	 * for it. For the MTP m1n1 only checks that it is powered and leaves
+	 * its filter as iBoot set it. The PMP is not requested: no J604 node
+	 * uses it yet.
+	 */
+	chosen {
+		linux-enablement-mac,dapf-handoff = "/arm-io/aop", "/arm-io/mtp";
+	};
+
 	led-controller {
 		compatible = "pwm-leds";
 		led-0 {
